  "notes": "Hyperpop-to-rave dance-pop, encoded on the rave mode the Lane line names first (120—135) with the pop mode inside the same band. The drums are authored from the entry word for word: \"hard-clipped four-on-the-floor kick, distorted clap on 2&4, and 16th hats; the hyperpop mode substitutes synthetic metallic/plastic percussion (the SOPHIE lineage) for a kit and uses white-noise sweeps as fills.\" So the kick sits on all four beats at near-zero syncopation, the clap stack is hard-transient with a zero offset, `hihat.base` is 16th, and the perc lanes are `impact`, `riser`, `cowbell` and `woodblock` — the synthetic-object palette — with the snare roll ladder switched off because the fills are noise sweeps rather than rolls. The low end is a synth, not an 808 — \"distorted saw or square sub locked to the kick, pumping in 8ths\" — so no `bass808` block is authored and the bassline is a kick mirror at 0.97 with 8th-note repeated-root cells. Harmony is \"4-bar minor loops\" with \"Eurodance-derived i—VI—III—VII colour in the rave mode\", which is the top-weighted family. Melody encodes \"chant-like, short-cell, low-register and repetitive by design, with pitched vocal chops used as a rhythmic instrument\" as a two-to-three-pitch motif, a low register, the highest `riff` contour weight in this batch, and a `bell_echo`/`arp` counter for the chops; \"dissonant layers are combined intentionally\" raises `halfStepDissonance`. ⚠ `confidence` is medium against the research's high-medium rating because it says \"tempo bands genre-derived\"; every drum probability, the humanize numbers and the harmonic detail are INFERRED. `bpm.mode` 127 is the midpoint of the stated rave band.",
